    "thm-cost-optimal-stratified-allocation": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-stratified-sampling-design-and-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(U,y)$ be a finite population as in\n[[def-finite-population-and-sampling-design]], and let\n$U_1,\\ldots,U_H$ be a finite partition of $U$ into nonempty strata. Write\n$N_h=|U_h|$ and $N=\\sum_{h=1}^H N_h$. Choose integers\n$1\\le n_h\\le N_h$. In stratum $h$, draw $R_h$ by SRSWOR of size $n_h$ as in\n[[def-simple-random-sampling-without-replacement]], independently across\nstrata. Thus the joint probability of a tuple $(r_1,\\ldots,r_H)$ is\n$$\\prod_{h=1}^H\\binom{N_h}{n_h}^{-1},$$\nand its sample in $U$ is $R=\\bigcup_{h=1}^H r_h$. This is the\n**stratified SRSWOR design**. The union map is one-to-one because the strata\nare disjoint; the support has $\\prod_h\\binom{N_h}{n_h}$ elements, so the\ndisplayed mass function sums to one.\n\nThe fixed mean in stratum $h$ is\n$$\\bar y_h=\\frac1{N_h}\\sum_{i\\in U_h}y_i.$$\nFor a noncertainty stratum ($n_h<N_h$), necessarily $N_h>1$, and its finite-\npopulation variance is\n$$S_h^2=\\frac1{N_h-1}\\sum_{i\\in U_h}(y_i-\\bar y_h)^2.$$\nFor every noncertainty stratum write $S_h=\\sqrt{S_h^2}$ for its nonnegative\npopulation standard deviation. For a realized sample, set\n$$\\bar y_{h,R}=\\frac1{n_h}\\sum_{i\\in R_h}y_i,\\qquad \\widehat T_{st}=\\sum_{h=1}^H N_h\\bar y_{h,R}.$$\nThe quantity $\\widehat T_{st}$ is the **stratified total estimator**. When\n$n_h=N_h$, the stratum is a certainty stratum and its contribution is the\nfixed total $N_h\\bar y_h$; no $S_h^2$ is needed for that term. The population\nvalues $y_i$ remain fixed: only the sample selections are random.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-stratified-total-unbiasedness-and-variance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(U,y)$ be a finite population with a partition and stratified SRSWOR\ndesign as in [[def-stratified-sampling-design-and-estimator]]. Under the\ndesign probability $P_d$, the stratified total estimator satisfies\n$$E_d[\\widehat T_{st}]=\\sum_{i\\in U}y_i.$$\nIts design variance is\n$$\\operatorname{Var}_d(\\widehat T_{st})=\\sum_{h:n_h<N_h}N_h^2\\left(1-\\frac{n_h}{N_h}\\right)\\frac{S_h^2}{n_h}.$$\nThus certainty strata contribute zero and require no within-stratum variance\nterm.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $a_h=N_hS_h>0$. Replacing $n_h$ by $x_h$ in [F2] gives $N_h^2(1-x_h/N_h)S_h^2/x_h=a_h^2/x_h-N_hS_h^2$. The subtracted and fixed-stratum terms do not vary with $x$, so it suffices to minimize $\\sum_{h\\in J}a_h^2/x_h$ subject to $\\sum_{h\\in J}c_hx_h=C^\\star$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Set $p_h=a_h/\\sqrt{x_h}$ and $q_h=\\sqrt{c_hx_h}$. Expanding and grouping unordered pairs gives $(\\sum_hp_h^2)(\\sum_hq_h^2)-(\\sum_hp_hq_h)^2=\\sum_{h<k}(p_h^2q_k^2+p_k^2q_h^2-2p_hp_kq_hq_k)=\\sum_{h<k}(p_hq_k-p_kq_h)^2\\ge0$. Since $\\sum_hq_h^2=C^\\star$ and $\\sum_hp_hq_h=\\sum_ha_h\\sqrt{c_h}$, the objective is at least $(\\sum_ha_h\\sqrt{c_h})^2/C^\\star$, with equality exactly when $a_h/(x_h\\sqrt{c_h})$ is constant.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The allocation $x_h^\\star=C^\\star(a_h/\\sqrt{c_h})/(\\sum_{k\\in J}a_k\\sqrt{c_k})$ is positive and satisfies $\\sum_hc_hx_h^\\star=C^\\star$. It makes $a_h/(x_h^\\star\\sqrt{c_h})$ constant and attains step 2.1's bound; the equality condition uniquely determines it. By step 1.1 it uniquely minimizes the relaxed variance and gives the stated formula.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The variable stratum set J is nonempty, each c_h and S_h is positive, and residual cost Cstar>0. An empty J gives cost0 and violates the constraint; the normalization sum_{h in J}N_h S_h sqrt(c_h) in Proof3.1 is therefore strictly positive.",
          "reviewed": {
            "upheld": true,
            "by": "owner-delegated-focused-boundary-review",
            "reason": "The variable stratum set J is nonempty, each c_h and S_h is positive, and residual cost Cstar>0. An empty J gives cost0 and violates the constraint; the normalization sum_{h in J}N_h S_h sqrt(c_h) in Proof3.1 is therefore strictly positive."
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement requires S_h>0 for each variable noncertainty stratum."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement includes singleton J; step 3.1 reduces to the only feasible x_h=C^star/c_h."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim optimizes a positive continuous cost allocation and does not include a degenerate sampling design."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No endpoint case occurs in the claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Proof3.1 specifies x_h=Cstar(N_h S_h/sqrt(c_h))/sum_k N_k S_k sqrt(c_k), with all costs and variances positive. This common finite normalization gives all coordinates simultaneously and proves the cost constraint, with no choice-function or arbitrary-index selection step."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Proof2.1: a minimizer attaining the global cost-constrained bound has a_h/(x_h sqrt(c_h)) constant. The constraint sum c_h x_h=Cstar fixes the unique normalizer and yields the displayed cost allocation in3.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Proof3.1 constructs the displayed positive cost allocation, verifies sum c_h x_h=Cstar, and makes the equality ratios from2.1 constant. Thus its objective attains the global lower bound, proving optimality."
        }
      ],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
